        "original_id": "11ecb502-5405-528b-9a4d-361d6baa61a7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jorge Amado, Sarah Caudwell, Sarah Cockburn\n- Roles: writer, barrister\n- Concepts: Brazilian modernism, detective stories, literary translation, film adaptation\n- Works: Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands\n\nFacts:\n- Jorge Amado was born on 10 August 1912 and died on 6 August 2001.\n- Jorge Amado, born Jorge Leal Amado de Faria, was a Brazilian writer of the modernist school.\n- Sarah Caudwell was the pseudonym of Sarah Cockburn, who was born on 27 May 1939 and died on 28 January 2000.\n- Sarah Cockburn was a British barrister and writer of detective stories.\n- Jorge Amado remained the best known of modern Brazilian writers.\n- Jorge Amado's work was translated into about 49 languages.\n- Jorge Amado's work was adapted for film, including Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands in 1978.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5f0a2ec5-1eb6-5cb7-8d5d-38470f106fda",
        "text": "Page: Jorge Amado. Sentence: Jorge Leal Amado de Faria (] , 10 August 1912 – 6 August 2001) was a Brazilian writer of the modernist school.\nPage: Jorge Amado. Sentence: He remains the best known of modern Brazilian writers, with his work having been translated into some 49 languages and popularized in film, notably \"Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands\" in 1978.\nPage: Sarah Caudwell. Sentence: Sarah Caudwell was the pseudonym of Sarah Cockburn (27 May 1939 – 28 January 2000), a British barrister and writer of detective stories.",
